    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d38">

                <pb facs="0058" n="46" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg46"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">859B.7962/50: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Godthaab</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Penfield</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Godthaab</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-12T09:00:00-03:00">April 12, 1941—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>46. Department’s 34, April 10, 1 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>] p.m.<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d38-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The instructions contained in section 5 of the Department’s telegram under
                        reference have been carried out.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Both Governors were understandably greatly disturbed and resentful over
                        the ultimatum-like manner in which the agreement was presented to them.
                        Svane’s resentment seems to be directed entirely against the Danish Minister
                        whose statement that immediate agreement was the only alternative to British
                        occupation he suspects is an exaggeration. Brun at Ivigtut without the
                        background I have been able to give Svane seems to consider the Department
                        as inexcusably lacking in regard for the Governors.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Neither Governor appears to find serious fault with the substance of the
                        agreement. Brun, as the one most responsible for the Governors’ request last
                        June that American forces be landed at Ivigtut,<anchor n="17"
                            xml:id="d38-f2"/> has always considered some arrangement similar to that
                        now concluded as inevitable and desirable. Svane considers such an agreement
                        much less undesirable than any other possible form of foreign influence in
                        Greenland but would prefer to avoid concluding, the agreement until
                        necessary to forestall occupation or other foreign intervention, a
                        contingency <pb facs="0059" n="47" xml:id="pg47"/> which he apparently would
                        be prepared to admit as inevitable only when faced with the <hi
                            rendition="#italic">fait accompli</hi>.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Penfield</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d38-f1" n="16" xml:id="d38-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in section 5 the Department gave instruction that copies of notes
                    exchanged in the agreement signed April 9 be made available to the Governor of
                    North Greenland and the Governor of South Greenland (859B.7962/49b).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d38-f2" n="17" xml:id="d38-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 34, June 13, 4 p.m., from the Consul at Godthaab, <ref
                        target="frus1940v02#pg_369">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1940, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 369</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
